"""Original classification teaching, diagnostic retrieval and activities."""
import sys
from pathlib import Path
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]))
from authoring_support import Author
A=Author(2)
def E(o,h,c1,c2,more='',loc=None):
    A.entry(o,h,[c1,c2],more,source=('SRC-OSB2E',loc,'paraphrase') if loc else None)
def C(o,st,p,a,g,pending=False):A.card(o,st,p,a,g,pending=pending,demand={'input':p,'inference_or_processing':a} if st in ('APP','INTERP') else None)
E('2.1-01','Grouping by shared features',
 'Classification places organisms into groups using features they share. A useful diagnostic feature separates one group from the others being considered.',
 'Use several relevant structural features rather than a single superficial resemblance such as colour or habitat.',
 'A bird and a bat both have wings, but feathers identify the bird and hair identifies the mammal. A whale lives in water yet is a mammal, so habitat is not a dependable guide to a group; structure is.\n\nWhen you classify, state the observed feature first and then use it to justify the group. An animal with six jointed legs and a body in three parts is placed with the insects because of those features, not because it was found on a leaf. Colour varies within a group and is weak evidence; the number of leg pairs is a structural feature and strong evidence.',loc='20.1 Organizing Life on Earth')
C('2.1-01','FEATURE','State the basis for grouping organisms in biological classification.','Features that the organisms share.',['1 mark: states that organisms are grouped by the features they share, such as body structure; habitat or colour alone earns no mark.'])
E('2.1-02','Two-part scientific names',
 'In the binomial system, a species name has two parts: the genus name followed by the specific name. The genus begins with a capital letter and the specific name with a lower-case letter.',
 'The complete two-part name identifies the species. Different species may share a genus, so the specific name alone is not a complete species name.',
 'Humans are Homo sapiens: Homo is the genus and sapiens is the specific name. The lion, Panthera leo, and the tiger, Panthera tigris, share the genus Panthera but are different species because their second names differ. In print both words are italicised; handwritten, each is underlined separately. Common names vary between languages, whereas a scientific name is shared worldwide.',loc='20.1 Organizing Life on Earth')
C('2.1-02','FEATURE','Name the first part of a two-part scientific species name.','The genus.',['1 mark: names the genus as the first part of the name.'])
E('2.1-03','A hierarchy of groups',
 'A hierarchical classification contains broad groups divided into progressively smaller groups: kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus and species.',
 'Organisms placed in the same smaller group share the broader groups above it, while organisms in one broad group need not belong to the same species.',
 'The lion shows the whole sequence: kingdom Animalia, phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Carnivora, family Felidae, genus Panthera, species Panthera leo. The tiger shares every level with the lion down to genus, and differs only at species.\n\nTwo species in one genus therefore share their family, but two organisms in one family may belong to different genera. Each step down holds fewer organisms that are more alike, so species is the narrowest level.',loc='20.1 Organizing Life on Earth')
C('2.1-03','FEATURE','Outline the hierarchy of classification by naming, in order from the broader group to the narrower group, its three most specific levels.','Family, genus, species.',['1 mark: family, written in first position as the broadest of the three levels.','1 mark: genus, written in second position.','1 mark: species, written in third position as the narrowest level. Each mark needs the level name in its correct position, so a name in the wrong position earns no mark.'])
C('2.1-03','WHY','Panthera leo (lion) and Panthera tigris (tiger) share a genus. Explain why they are still different species.','A genus is a broader group that can contain several species. A species is identified by the whole two-part name, and the specific names leo and tigris differ, so they are different species.',['1 mark: gives the reason that a genus is a broader group that can contain more than one species.','1 mark: states that the specific names differ (leo and tigris), and a species is identified by the whole two-part name.'])
E('2.1-04','Constructing and using dichotomous keys',
 'A dichotomous key uses successive pairs of contrasting statements, with one applicable choice at each step. Each choice leads to another pair or to an identification.',
 'Use visible, unambiguous features and make the two choices at a step mutually exclusive; follow the route from the beginning for each specimen.',
 'Take four animals: an earthworm, a snail, a spider and an ant. The first pair of statements is 1a jointed legs absent, go to 2; 1b jointed legs present, go to 3. At step 2, 2a body divided into ring-like segments gives earthworm and 2b body not divided into segments gives snail. At step 3, 3a four pairs of legs gives spider and 3b three pairs of legs gives ant. Each animal follows exactly one route, and no route ends in a dead end.\n\n“Large” and “small” are unreliable unless a measurement is given; “longer than 2 cm” works. Use only features you can see or that you are given. Check a key by following each organism’s route to its name.\n\nCommon faults are overlapping choices, such as “legs” and “many legs”, a statement with no matching pair, and a route that reaches two names. Questions may ask you to build a key from a table or to follow a given key.')
table='| Specimen | Jointed legs | Leg pairs | Antennae pairs |\n|---|---|---:|---:|\n| A | absent | 0 | 0 |\n| B | present | 3 | 1 |\n| C | present | 4 | 0 |\n| D | present | 5 | 2 |'
C('2.1-04','APP','Construct a dichotomous key to identify specimens A to D, using only the observations in this table.\n'+table,
 'One possible key: 1a Jointed legs absent → A; 1b Jointed legs present → 2. 2a Two pairs of antennae → D; 2b Fewer than two pairs of antennae → 3. 3a Three pairs of legs → B; 3b Four pairs of legs → C. Any key that separates all four specimens correctly is credited.',
 ['1 mark: each step gives two contrasting statements, for example legs present or absent.','1 mark: the statements use only features given in the table.','1 mark: the key leads from the first step to a named endpoint for each of A, B, C and D.','1 mark: each specimen reaches one identification only, because the choices cannot overlap and there is no dead end.'])
key='1a Jointed legs absent → A\n1b Jointed legs present → go to 2\n2a Antennae absent → C\n2b Antennae present → go to 3\n3a One pair of antennae → B\n3b Two pairs of antennae → D'
C('2.1-04','INTERP','Use this key to name the letter reached by each of four animals. (i) no jointed legs; (ii) jointed legs, no antennae; (iii) jointed legs, one pair of antennae; (iv) jointed legs, two pairs of antennae.\n'+key,
 '(i) A; (ii) C; (iii) B; (iv) D.',
 ['1 mark: (i) A, reading jointed legs absent at step 1a, so the key ends there.','1 mark: (ii) C, reading jointed legs present at 1b and antennae absent at 2a.','1 mark: (iii) B, reading antennae present at 2b and one pair of antennae at 3a.','1 mark: (iv) D, reading two pairs of antennae at 3b.'])
E('2.2-01','Virus structure',
 'A virus has genetic material enclosed by a protein coat. It is non-cellular: it is not made of cells and is not a single cell.',
 'Inside a host cell, the virus genetic material carries the instructions used to make new virus particles. The protein coat surrounds and protects that material.',
 'A virus has no cytoplasm and no cell membrane. Even a bacterium, which has no nucleus, has both, so a virus is not a cell of any kind. “Non-cellular” does not mean a very small single-celled organism: a virus is far smaller than a bacterium and built on a different plan. In a simple drawing, show the genetic material in the centre and the coat as an outline around it. The coat is protein, not a cellulose cell wall.',loc='21.1 Viral Evolution, Morphology, and Classification')
C('2.2-01','FEATURE','Outline the structure of a virus. State how this differs from the structure of a single-celled organism.','Genetic material is enclosed in a protein coat. A virus is non-cellular: it has no cell structure, such as cytoplasm or a cell membrane, whereas a single-celled organism, such as a bacterium, is one complete cell.',['1 mark: names the genetic material inside the virus.','1 mark: names the protein coat that surrounds it.','1 mark: states the difference, that a virus is non-cellular, with no cell structure, whereas a single-celled organism is one complete cell. Saying only that a virus has no nucleus earns no mark, because bacteria have no nucleus either.'])
E('2.2-02','Are viruses living?',
 'Viruses carry genetic material and are replicated within suitable host cells, which supplies arguments for associating them with life.',
 'They lack cellular organisation and independent metabolic activity, and cannot be replicated without host cells; these support treating them as non-living.',
 'Name specific features: for, a virus carries genetic material and is copied inside a host cell; against, it does not respire, feed or grow by itself.\n\nThe judgement depends on the characteristics used to define a living organism. Because a virus meets few of them without a host, it is usually treated as a borderline case rather than a typical living thing.',loc='21.1 Viral Evolution, Morphology, and Classification')
C('2.2-02','EVAL','Discuss one argument for and two arguments against classifying viruses as living organisms.','For: a virus carries genetic material, and it reproduces inside host cells. Against: a virus is non-cellular, and it does not respire, feed or grow by itself. Overall, viruses are usually treated as a borderline case.',['1 mark: gives one argument for: carries genetic material, or reproduces inside host cells.','1 mark: gives one argument against from: non-cellular; does not respire or carry out its own metabolism; does not feed, grow or excrete by itself.','1 mark: gives a second, different argument against from the same list. Needing a host cell to reproduce can be credited on one side only, not as both a for and an against point. No separate mark is given for an overall judgement.'])
groups=[
 ('03','Monocotyledons','Monocotyledon seeds have one cotyledon; their leaves commonly have parallel veins.','Their flowers commonly have parts in threes or multiples of three, and their root system is typically fibrous.','Grasses and maize show this combination.','List two leaf or root features typical of monocotyledons.','Parallel leaf veins and a fibrous root system.',['1 mark: names parallel leaf veins as one feature.','1 mark: names a fibrous root system as the second feature.'],'26.3 Angiosperms'),
 ('04','Dicotyledons','Dicotyledon seeds have two cotyledons; their leaves commonly have a branching network of veins.','Flower parts commonly occur in fours or fives or their multiples, and a taproot with lateral branches is typical.','A bean plant is a familiar example.','List two leaf or root features typical of dicotyledons.','Network leaf veins and a taproot with side branches.',['1 mark: names a branching network of leaf veins as one feature.','1 mark: names a taproot with side branches as the second feature.'],'26.3 Angiosperms'),
 ('05','Molluscs','Molluscs have a soft, unsegmented body, and many have a shell that protects it.','A muscular foot is a characteristic structure; shell form and presence vary among members.','A snail shows a shell and a muscular foot. A slug has no external shell but is still a mollusc.','List two external features useful for identifying a snail as a mollusc.','A soft, unsegmented body and a muscular foot. A shell is also accepted for a snail, although some molluscs, such as slugs, have none.',['1 mark: names one creditable feature from: a soft, unsegmented body (one point); a muscular foot; a shell, which a snail has although not every mollusc does.', '1 mark: names a second, different feature from the same list; the same idea in other words earns no further mark.'],None),
 ('06','Annelids','Annelids have a body divided into repeated ring-like segments.','The body is elongated and lacks jointed legs; earthworms also have small bristles used in movement.','The rings of an earthworm are true repeated segments, not surface colour bands. An earthworm cannot be mistaken for a myriapod, because it has no jointed legs.','List two external features of an earthworm useful in classification.','A long body of repeated ring-like segments, and no jointed legs. Small bristles are also accepted.',['1 mark: names one creditable feature from: a body of repeated ring-like segments; no jointed legs; small bristles.', '1 mark: names a second, different feature from the same list; the same idea in other words earns no further mark.'],None),
 ('07','Insects','An insect has three body regions: head, thorax and abdomen.','It has three pairs of jointed legs and one pair of antennae; wings occur in many insects but are not a requirement for the group.','Insects, arachnids, crustaceans and myriapods are all arthropods: animals with jointed legs, a segmented body and a hard external skeleton called an exoskeleton.\n\nAn ant is an insect even when it has no wings. Count the legs attached to the thorax, and do not mistake antennae for legs.','List two diagnostic external features of an adult insect.','Three pairs of jointed legs and a body in three regions (head, thorax and abdomen). One pair of antennae is also accepted.',['1 mark: names one creditable feature from: three pairs of jointed legs; a body in three regions; one pair of antennae.', '1 mark: names a second, different feature from the same list; the same idea in other words earns no further mark.'],None),
 ('08','Arachnids','Arachnids have four pairs of jointed legs and no antennae.','Their body is commonly described as two main regions, rather than the head, thorax and abdomen of insects.','A spider shows the eight-legged arrangement. Scorpions and ticks are arachnids too.','List two external features distinguishing a spider from an insect.','Four pairs of legs and no antennae. Two body regions is also accepted.',['1 mark: names one creditable feature from: four pairs of legs; no antennae; two main body regions.', '1 mark: names a second, different feature from the same list; the same idea in other words earns no further mark.'],None),
 ('09','Crustaceans','Crustaceans have two pairs of antennae and more than four pairs of jointed legs.','A hard external covering protects the body, and the main body regions are typically a joined head and thorax region and an abdomen.','Crabs, shrimps, woodlice and water fleas are crustaceans. Count antennae and leg pairs rather than judging by shape: a woodlouse has seven pairs of legs and two pairs of antennae, so it is a crustacean although it lives on land.','List two external features useful for identifying a typical crustacean.','Two pairs of antennae and more than four pairs of jointed legs.',['1 mark: names two pairs of antennae as one feature.','1 mark: names more than four pairs of jointed legs as the second feature.'],None),
 ('10','Myriapods','Myriapods have an elongated body made of many segments, with many jointed legs.','They have one pair of antennae; centipedes have one pair of legs on each segment and millipedes have two.','An earthworm has no jointed legs, whereas centipedes and millipedes have many, so they are arthropods, not annelids.','List two external features useful for identifying a myriapod.','Many body segments and many jointed legs.',['1 mark: names a long body of many segments as one feature.','1 mark: names many jointed legs, one or two pairs on each segment, as the second feature.'],None),
 ('11','Fish','Fish have fins and a streamlined body suited to movement through water.','They obtain oxygen through gills. Most fish have scales on the skin, but a few, such as catfish, have none.','Fins and gills are the most dependable features; in bony fish the gills lie under a gill cover (operculum). A lateral line runs along each side.','List two features commonly used to classify a fish.','Fins and gills. Scales, a gill cover (operculum) or a lateral line are also accepted.',['1 mark: names one creditable feature from: fins; gills or a gill cover (operculum), as one point; scales on the skin, which most fish have; a lateral line.', '1 mark: names a second, different feature from the same list; the same idea in other words earns no further mark.'],None),
 ('12','Amphibians','Amphibians typically have moist skin without the dry scales of reptiles.','Familiar adult forms commonly have four limbs; their larvae usually live in water and differ from adults.','A frog has smooth, moist skin and begins life as a tadpole. Moist skin is more useful than living near water, because habitat alone does not separate amphibians from aquatic reptiles.','List two external features typical of an adult frog.','Moist skin without scales, and four limbs.',['1 mark: names moist skin without scales as one feature; moist and scaleless together count as one point only.','1 mark: names four limbs as the second feature.'],None),
 ('13','Reptiles','Reptiles have dry skin covered with scales.','A typical reptile such as a lizard has clawed toes, and most reptiles lay eggs with leathery shells on land. Snakes have no limbs, so having four limbs is not a requirement of the group.','A lizard and a snake differ in limb presence but share the dry scaly covering. A frog, by contrast, has moist skin and lays jelly-covered eggs in water.','List two features of a typical lizard that separate reptiles from amphibians.','Dry skin covered with scales, and eggs with leathery shells laid on land. Clawed toes are also accepted.',['1 mark: names dry skin covered with scales as one feature; dry and scaly together count as one point only.','1 mark: names a second feature: clawed toes, or eggs with leathery shells laid on land.'],None),
 ('14','Birds','Birds have feathers and wings.','Their forelimbs form the wings, and they lay eggs with hard shells. Flight ability varies, so flight itself is not a reliable defining feature.','An ostrich is a bird although it does not fly: it still has feathers and wings. A bat has wings but no feathers, so feathers identify a bird. A beak does not, because turtles and octopuses also have beaks.','List two visible external features of a bird.','Feathers, and forelimbs formed as wings. A beak is also accepted.',['1 mark: names feathers, a feature found only in birds.','1 mark: names a second feature: forelimbs formed as wings, or a beak.'],None),
 ('15','Mammals','Every mammal has hair or fur at some stage of life, and females have mammary glands that produce milk.','Visible external features include hair or fur, external ears (pinnae) and whiskers. Milk and live birth are not visible external features.','A whale is a mammal despite living in water: it has sparse hair and feeds its young on milk. Limbs and habitat vary between mammals.','List two features used to classify mammals.','Hair or fur at some life stage; milk production by mammary glands in females. External ears (pinnae) are also accepted.',['1 mark: names hair or fur as one feature.','1 mark: names a second feature: mammary glands producing milk in females, or external ears (pinnae).'],None),
]
for num,h,c1,c2,more,p,a,points,loc in groups:
 o='2.2-'+num;E(o,h,c1,c2,more,loc=loc)
 C(o,'FEATURE',p,a,points)
E('2.2-16','Observing and drawing local organisms',
 'Observe a locally available organism carefully and select diagnostic features that can actually be seen, recording its shape and relative proportions.',
 'Make a large clear line drawing with accurate labels ending on visible structures. Draw what the organism looks like, not an idealised version remembered from a book.',
 'Handle living animals gently and return them to where they were found.\n\nTake a sharp pencil and draw a continuous single-line outline with no shading. Check proportions by comparing lengths, for example the abdomen of an ant against its thorax. Add label lines with a ruler: they should be straight, should not cross and should end exactly on the structure. Write labels at the side in horizontal text. Give the drawing a title naming the organism. Label features that show the group, such as the three pairs of legs of an insect. You may instead be asked for adaptive features: label each with its use, such as the thick waxy cuticle that cuts water loss in a desert plant, or a beetle’s hard wing cases that protect its body.')
C('2.2-16','APP','Observe a common local invertebrate animal, such as a beetle, an ant, a woodlouse or a snail. Draw a labelled line drawing of it, showing its relative proportions, and label at least two features that show which group it belongs to.','A line drawing with a clear, continuous outline and no shading; the main parts in realistic relative sizes; ruled label lines that end on the structures; at least two labels naming features that show the group. For an ant: three body regions, three pairs of legs on the thorax and one pair of antennae, labelled head, thorax, abdomen, leg and antenna.',['1 mark: the outline is drawn in clear, continuous lines with no shading.','1 mark: the relative sizes of the main parts match this specimen as observed, for example an ant\u2019s abdomen compared with its thorax.','1 mark: label lines are ruled and each one ends on the structure it names.','1 mark: at least two labels name features that place the animal in its group, because these are the diagnostic features, for example three pairs of legs or three body regions for an insect.'])
C('2.2-09','MISCON','An animal has five pairs of jointed legs and two pairs of antennae. A learner says it must be an arachnid, because it has more legs than an insect. Explain the error.','An arachnid has exactly four pairs of legs and no antennae, so an animal with antennae cannot be an arachnid. Two pairs of antennae and more than four pairs of legs are crustacean features, so the animal is a crustacean.',['1 mark: names the mistake: an arachnid has four pairs of legs and no antennae, so the two pairs of antennae rule an arachnid out.','1 mark: states that the animal is instead a crustacean, because two pairs of antennae and more than four pairs of legs are crustacean features.'])
q=[
('Which two parts form a binomial name?',['Family and class','Genus and specific name','Order and phylum','Common name and habitat'],'B','B: a binomial name is the genus followed by the specific name. A and C are levels of the hierarchy, and D is not part of a scientific name.'),
('Which of these groups is the narrowest?',['Kingdom','Family','Genus','Species'],'D','D: species is the narrowest level. Kingdom, family and genus each contain more organisms.'),
('Which of these is non-cellular?',['A virus','An insect','A fungus cell','A mammalian cell'],'A','A: a virus is not made of cells. B, C and D are cells or are made of cells.'),
('What encloses viral genetic material?',['Cellulose wall','A nucleus','Protein coat','Muscular foot'],'C','C: a protein coat encloses viral genetic material. A and B are parts of cells, and D is a mollusc structure.'),
('Which feature is typical of monocotyledons?',['Two cotyledons','Network veins','Fibrous roots','Flower parts mainly in fives'],'C','C: monocotyledons have fibrous roots. A, B and D are dicotyledon features.'),
('Which feature most clearly identifies a bird?',['Flight','Feathers','Four limbs','Living on land'],'B','B: only birds have feathers. Some birds cannot fly, and four limbs and living on land are shared with other groups.'),
('Which arthropod group has no antennae?',['Typical insect','Typical crustacean','Typical arachnid','Typical myriapod'],'C','C: arachnids have no antennae. Insects and myriapods have one pair and crustaceans two pairs.'),
('Which is a reliable reptile feature?',['Dry scales','Four legs in every member','Feathers','Moist scaleless skin'],'A','A: every reptile has dry, scaly skin. Snakes have no legs, feathers belong to birds and moist scaleless skin to amphibians.'),
('An unfamiliar specimen has three leg pairs, one antenna pair and three body regions. Which group fits?',['Arachnid','Annelid','Insect','Crustacean'],'C','C: an insect has all three features. Arachnids have four pairs of legs, annelids have no jointed legs and crustaceans have two pairs of antennae.'),
('A plant has network leaf veins and flower parts in fives. Which additional feature is most consistent?',['One cotyledon','Two cotyledons','Two antenna pairs','Feathers'],'B','B: network veins and flower parts in fives are dicotyledon features, and dicotyledons have two cotyledons. A is a monocotyledon feature, and C and D are animal features.'),
('A key first separates jointed legs present from absent. Which choice applies to an earthworm?',['Present','Absent','Both','Neither because it moves'],'B','B: an earthworm has no jointed legs. Being able to move says nothing about whether it has legs.'),
('Two organisms share a genus but have different complete species names. What follows?',['Same species','Different kingdoms','Different species within the genus','No shared family'],'C','C: a genus can hold several species. Different specific names mean different species, and organisms in one genus share their kingdom and family.')]
prompt='\n\n'.join(f'{n}. {p}\n'+'\n'.join(f'{chr(65+k)}. {v}' for k,v in enumerate(opts)) for n,(p,opts,key,why) in enumerate(q,1))
answer='\n'.join(f'{n}: {key}. {why}' for n,(p,opts,key,why) in enumerate(q,1))
A.task('multiple_choice_set',['2.1-02','2.1-03','2.2-01','2.2-03','2.2-04','2.2-07','2.2-08','2.2-09','2.2-13','2.2-14','2.1-04'],prompt,answer,['1 mark for each question answered with the correct option (the key), twelve marks in all.','A wrong option (distractor), or more than one option chosen, earns no mark for that question.'],12,['AO1','AO2'])
A.task('short_answer',['2.1-02','2.1-03','2.2-07','2.2-09'],
 'Specimen P has three pairs of legs and one pair of antennae. Specimen Q has five pairs of legs and two pairs of antennae. Suppose a beetle is given the scientific name Genora alba.\n(a) Name P’s group. [1]\n(b) Name Q’s group. [1]\n(c) State which part of Genora alba is the genus and which is the specific name. [2]\n(d) Using the descriptions of P and Q, explain why they should not be put in the same arthropod group. [2]\n(e) Suggest why a shared brown colour would be weaker classification evidence than the appendage counts. [2]',
 '(a) Insect. (b) Crustacean. (c) Genora is the genus; alba is the specific name. (d) P has three pairs of legs whereas Q has five, and P has one pair of antennae whereas Q has two, so they have different diagnostic features. (e) Colour can vary within a group and can be shared by unrelated animals; leg and antenna counts are structural features that fall in a set range for each group and differ between groups.',
 ['One creditable point earns each mark. (a) 1 mark: insect for P. (b) 1 mark: crustacean for Q.','(c) 1 mark: Genora is the genus. 1 mark: alba is the specific name.','(d) 1 mark: contrasts the leg pairs, three for P and five for Q. 1 mark: contrasts the antenna pairs, one for P and two for Q.','(e) 1 mark: says colour can vary within a group or be shared by unrelated animals. 1 mark: says leg and antenna counts are structural features that fall in a set range for each group and so distinguish groups.'],8,['AO1','AO2'],'name')
A.task('practical_task',['2.2-07','2.2-09'],
 'A learner samples equal 1 m² patches of leaf litter in open and shaded positions, with the same search time of five minutes per patch. The counts of animals with jointed legs are example data.\n\n| Position | Patch 1 / number | Patch 2 / number | Patch 3 / number |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Open | 3 | 4 | 5 |\n| Shaded | 8 | 10 | 12 |\n\n(a) Identify the comparison factor and the measured response. [2]\n(b) Calculate the mean count in each position. [2]\n(c) Describe the comparison supported by these data. [1]\n(d) State one other variable, not already given, that should be kept the same in the method. [1]\n(e) Some animals hide deep in the litter. Suggest how missing them could bias the comparison between open and shaded patches, and suggest one improvement. [2]\n(f) Animal A has three pairs of jointed legs and one pair of antennae. Animal B has seven pairs of jointed legs and two pairs of antennae. Name the arthropod group of each animal. [2]',
 '(a) Position, open or shaded; count of animals found per patch in five minutes. (b) Open mean 4, shaded mean 10. (c) More were found in shaded patches in this sample. (d) Depth of leaf litter, time of day, or the same person searching. (e) If animals hide more easily in one position, for example in thicker litter in the shade, a larger share would be missed there, so the difference between the means would be wrong. Improvement: sift all the litter from each patch thoroughly, for example on a white tray, so hidden animals are found in both positions. (f) A is an insect; B is a crustacean.',
 ['(a) 1 mark for naming position, open or shaded, as the variable being compared. 1 mark for naming the count of animals as the measured response.','(b) 1 mark for each correct mean from the table data: open 4, shaded 10.','(c) 1 mark for the trend that more animals were found in the shade; accept a supporting comparison such as means of 4 and 10.','(d) 1 mark for one other variable kept the same in the method, such as litter depth, time of day or the same searcher.','(e) 1 mark for the limitation that hidden animals may be missed more in one position than the other, which distorts the comparison. 1 mark for an improvement that finds hidden animals, such as sifting all the litter thoroughly.','(f) 1 mark: A is an insect. 1 mark: B is a crustacean.'],10,['AO3'],'identify')
A.notes['deviations'].append({'from':'P03 previously claimed the local-observation-and-drawing objective 2.2-16 without asking for a drawing.','to':'P03 now maps to the insect and crustacean feature objectives 2.2-07 and 2.2-09, both tested in part (f); the sampling and means use Topic 1 practical skills as prerequisites.','why':'Keep the Paper 3 practical comparison while removing a false drawing claim; a separate held item still covers objective 2.2-16.'})
